{"statements":{"L3.1":{"established":"yes","quote":"The two players' totals sum to $1$. Therefore Liu Bang's value in the claiming stage is
\[
\frac{1+D}{2}. \tag{3}
\]
Thus it remains to prove that the minimax value of $D$ in the marking stage is $d$.","note":"Backward induction proves the alternating-discrepancy reduction for every final multiset."},"L3.2":{"established":"yes","quote":"When put in nondecreasing order, two consecutive subset sums differ by at most
\[
\frac1{2^{n+1}-1}=d. \tag{11}
\]
Choose distinct subsets $S,T$ giving such a pair, and cancel their intersection.","note":"Pigeonhole, cancellation, common refinement, and the at-most-n cut count are fully proved."},"L3.3":{"established":"yes","quote":"The integer in parentheses is nonzero: in a nonempty signed sum of distinct powers of two, the largest power is greater than the sum of all smaller powers. Its absolute value is therefore at least $1$.","note":"The weighted-tree argument handles every Xiang reply and derives D at least d."}},"S_ref":2,"notes":"Both universal bounds and the claiming-stage reduction are rigorously established for all positive integers n; the stated closed form follows."}
